## DESCRIPTION
# A problem that asks students to set up a hypothesis test. 
## ENDDESCRIPTION 
# This problem is derived from a homework problem in Introductory Statistics,
# licensed by OpenStax under a Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY 4.0).
# Modified for WeBWorK by Michael Stassen   mstassen(at)fitchburgstate(dot)edu


## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Hypothesis tests)
## DBsection(One sample proportion)
## KEYWORDS('inference', 'hypothesis test', 'voting')
## Author('Michael Stassen')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros ('PGstandard.pl',  "PGML.pl","parserPopUp.pl", 'PGCourse.pl');

Context("LimitedNumeric")->variables->are(p=>'Real');

$p = random(41,49);

@opts = ('\(=\)', '\(\neq\)', '\(<\)', '\(>\)');
$dd1 = DropDown(~~@opts,0,useHTMLSelect => 0);
$dd2 = DropDown(~~@opts,3,useHTMLSelect => 0);

$popup = PopUp(["","left-tailed","right-tailed","two-tailed"],"right-tailed");    

BEGIN_PGML
The statement below is a testable claim about a population parameter. Write the
null and alternative hypotheses in terms of [`p`].

Enter percents as decimals, without a leading 0. For example, if one of your 
hypotheses were that [`p`] is 50%, enter that as as *p=.50*.  

More than [$p]\% of voters support Mayor Sam Squailia.

- [`H_0`]: [_]{Compute('p')}[_]{$dd1} [____]{$p/100}
- [`H_a`]: [_]{Compute('p')}[_]{$dd2} [____]{$p/100}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
- The null hypothesis is always the neutral statement and in this case, it is [` p = [$p/100] `]
- Since the statement asks for "more than [$p]%", then the alternative is [`>`] or the statement is [`p > [$p/100] `].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
